1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
>> So real quick
before we continue,

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:03,480
I want to comment on how AI

3
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,620
can play into the
design life cycle.

4
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:07,860
There are three primary
ways that I see

5
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:10,320
AI being an asset
in this process.

6
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,000
First, AI can be
used to jump start

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,120
getting a baseline for
user perspectives.

8
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,600
If you're working in
a totally new field,

9
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,520
it might be a good
idea to say to AI,

10
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,040
Hey, pretend like you're
a user in this area.

11
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:25,100
What are your needs
most likely to be?

12
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,060
Depending on the task, it
might be able to give you

13
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:31,420
some decent initial ideas for
what you want to explore.

14
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:33,200
You'll still need
to explore those in

15
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,960
more detail with the real users,

16
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,860
but at least it can
give you a head start

17
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,405
so you're not going in
completely clueless.

18
00:00:39,405 --> 00:00:41,670
We do have to be
careful here, though,

19
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:44,970
because this can have both
an upside and a downside.

20
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:48,170
On the positive side,
it can prime you with

21
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:50,270
the vocabulary users
in your demand likely

22
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:51,610
use so you can make

23
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:53,870
sure you're asking
the right questions.

24
00:00:53,870 --> 00:00:56,250
But on the negative side, it

25
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,585
might constrain your
thinking too early.

26
00:00:58,585 --> 00:01:01,770
You might focus so hard on the
initial ideas that AI gave

27
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:03,350
you that you don't ask

28
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:04,550
broad enough questions to see

29
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:06,350
if there's more than
what it suggested.

30
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:08,550
So be careful and

31
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:11,350
question its assumptions
as you're going through.

32
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:14,210
Second, AI can be a great tool

33
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:16,910
to practice with as
if it's your user.

34
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:18,690
You can give your survey to AI

35
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:20,570
and ask you to
generate 20 responses,

36
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:22,190
then see if those
are the types of

37
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:24,510
responses you hope to
get from real users.

38
00:01:24,510 --> 00:01:27,550
Or you can rehearse
giving AI instructions on

39
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:28,850
using your interface to

40
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:31,070
see if it understands
what you're asking.

41
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:33,270
In a lot of ways, this starts

42
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:35,360
to be like rubber
duck debugging.

43
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:37,110
Rubber duck debugging is an idea

44
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:38,830
from coding where you
figure out the issues

45
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:40,270
with your code by
explaining it to

46
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:42,600
someone else, perhaps
a rubber duck.

47
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,990
Here, AI is a sophisticated
version of that.

48
00:01:46,990 --> 00:01:49,670
You practice your survey
or your interview by

49
00:01:49,670 --> 00:01:51,390
surveying or interviewing
a rubber duck

50
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:53,050
that just happens to talk back.

51
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:54,890
You wouldn't put a lot of stake

52
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:56,550
in the content of what it says,

53
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,190
but it lets you practice
what you're going to ask and

54
00:01:59,190 --> 00:02:02,505
how you're going to respond
to different replies.

55
00:02:02,505 --> 00:02:05,570
We can use this on the
evaluation side as well.

56
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:08,850
Plan out your evaluation
and ask an AI to generate

57
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:10,230
some fake data that could have

58
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:12,430
come from the evaluation
you're proposing.

59
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:16,110
Analyze it as if it's the
real thing to see if you're

60
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:17,210
going to be able to answer

61
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:20,150
your research questions
once you get the real data.

62
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:22,050
I've done this before and

63
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:23,770
it's helped me realize
that I needed to

64
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:25,450
collect data I
hadn't planned on if

65
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:28,150
I wanted to answer the
questions I have in mind.

66
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:30,990
Whether AI is standing in for

67
00:02:30,990 --> 00:02:32,490
a single real user or

68
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:35,110
generating the data for
dozens of pretend users,

69
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:36,490
it can be a great way to

70
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:39,835
practice before you start
to talk to real people.

71
00:02:39,835 --> 00:02:43,220
Third, there are AI
tools out there that

72
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:44,360
really help you accomplish

73
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,720
certain parts of the
design life cycle faster.

74
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,120
Prototyping is a big one.

75
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:50,960
Later in this unit, we're

76
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:52,860
going to talk about
paper prototyping.

77
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:55,380
Paper prototyping is
drawing out an interface on

78
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:56,920
paper so you can get feedback

79
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,220
from potential
users super quick.

80
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:01,760
But paper prototyping
also dates back to

81
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:04,100
a time when the
only way to create

82
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:06,500
a real prototype was
to use a bunch of

83
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,000
really frustrating libraries in

84
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:09,735
the programming language
of your choice.

85
00:03:09,735 --> 00:03:12,800
Building a real UI
took a lot of time,

86
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:14,940
so you wanted to make sure
you liked what you were

87
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,675
building before you got started.

88
00:03:17,675 --> 00:03:19,530
But AI has made it a lot

89
00:03:19,530 --> 00:03:21,750
easier to build good
prototypes fast.

90
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:23,770
This isn't specific to AI.

91
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:25,650
There's a long
history of developing

92
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:28,930
new tools to make prototyping
faster and easier.

93
00:03:28,930 --> 00:03:31,710
But AI is the latest
iteration of this.

94
00:03:31,710 --> 00:03:33,910
As long as you're not
letting whatever tools

95
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:36,330
you're using overconstrain
what you're building,

96
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:38,690
they can help you build
sophisticated prototypes

97
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:42,270
fast so that you can get to
useful feedback even faster.

98
00:03:42,270 --> 00:03:43,830
Now there's something really

99
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:45,670
important to emphasize
here though.

100
00:03:45,670 --> 00:03:49,430
At no point during any
of this did I ever

101
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:53,670
suggest using AI instead
of interacting with users?

102
00:03:53,670 --> 00:03:57,050
Nothing can replace
real user feedback.

103
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:59,070
AI is not your user,

104
00:03:59,070 --> 00:04:02,070
and so getting information
from AI is never

105
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:05,870
a substitute for getting
information from real users.

106
00:04:05,870 --> 00:04:08,440
But what AI can do is help you

107
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,080
make the most of the time
you do have with users.

108
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,200
It can help you get
an early baseline

109
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:14,680
for what answers to expect.

110
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,700
It can help you
practice your questions

111
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:19,300
and make sure you're likely
to get useful answers.

112
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:21,360
It can help you
create prototypes

113
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,660
that you can get in front
of users more quickly.

114
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:26,500
AI doesn't replace
anything that you do,

115
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:29,680
but it can help you
do it a lot faster.
